1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,555
This video is about one fact. Every regular language has exactly one minimal D F A, up to the names of the states.

2
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:24,770
We use a map between two machines, called a homomorphism. It shows that two machines agree. Then we show why the minimal D F A is the smallest one. Part 2 uses these facts to decide if two regexes are the same.

3
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:36,548
So far we have the full pipeline. Templates give an N F A. The subset construction gives a D F A. Moore's algorithm gives the minimal D F A.

4
00:00:36,998 --> 00:00:45,851
Now a new question. Two different D F As accept the same language. How are they related? Do they give the same minimal machine?

5
00:00:46,301 --> 00:00:57,949
First, homomorphisms. A homomorphism is a map from the states of one D F A to the states of another. It proves that the two machines accept the same strings.

6
00:00:58,399 --> 00:01:05,205
Second, why the minimal D F A is the smallest. No D F A for the language can have fewer states.

7
00:01:05,655 --> 00:01:12,033
Third, deciding if two regexes are the same, and the cost of each step. That is part 2.

8
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:22,638
At the top is our D F A for a or a b star, from the subset construction. It has four states. B and C accept. D is the error state.

9
00:01:23,088 --> 00:01:31,600
Below is the minimal machine from Moore's algorithm. Moore merged B and C into one state, B C. It has three states.

10
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:44,018
The dashed arrows are a map. We call it h. Each state of our D F A goes to the state that it became. A goes to A. D goes to D. B and C both go to B C.

11
00:01:44,468 --> 00:01:51,807
Now compare the arrows of the two machines. Take one arrow at the top, and follow h from both of its ends.

12
00:01:52,257 --> 00:02:05,889
B reads b and goes to C. h sends B to B C, and h sends C to B C. Below, B C reads b and goes to B C. So the step is still there. It is the loop on B C.

13
00:02:06,339 --> 00:02:11,160
Another arrow. C reads a and goes to D. Which step is that below?

14
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:20,143
h sends C to B C, and D to D. Below, B C reads a and goes to D. Again the arrow is there, with the same letter.

15
00:02:20,593 --> 00:02:29,063
Check all eight arrows at the top, and you find the same result. Every arrow up top lands on an arrow below with the same letter.

16
00:02:29,513 --> 00:02:34,889
Now run a string on both machines. Take a b b. Where does each machine end?

17
00:02:35,339 --> 00:02:38,624
Both machines start in A. And h of A is A.

18
00:02:39,074 --> 00:02:44,685
Read a. The top goes to B. The machine below goes to B C, which is h of B.

19
00:02:45,135 --> 00:02:51,087
Read b. The top goes to C. Below, B C loops to B C, which is h of C.

20
00:02:51,537 --> 00:03:03,078
Read the last b. The top stays in C, and the machine below stays in B C. Both states accept. At each step, the state below is h of the state at the top.

21
00:03:03,528 --> 00:03:10,056
Here are the three things that we saw in the picture. First, the start state goes to the start state.

22
00:03:10,506 --> 00:03:22,517
Second, every step up top is a step below, with the same letter. Go right on x and then down with h. Or go down with h and then right on x. You arrive at the same state.

23
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:29,217
Third, accepting states go to accepting states, and rejecting states go to rejecting states.

24
00:03:29,667 --> 00:03:42,723
So for every string, the run below is h of the run up top, step by step. One run ends in an accepting state exactly when the other does. The two machines accept the same strings.

25
00:03:43,173 --> 00:03:47,035
A map with these three properties is called a homomorphism.

26
00:03:47,485 --> 00:04:00,455
Now the same three lines, formally. M and M prime are two D F As over the same alphabet. A homomorphism h is a map from the states of M to the states of M prime.

27
00:04:00,905 --> 00:04:06,964
Line 1. h of the start state q zero is the start state q zero prime.

28
00:04:07,414 --> 00:04:18,721
Line 2. For every state q and letter x, h of delta of q and x equals delta prime of h of q and x. Reading a letter commutes with h.

29
00:04:19,171 --> 00:04:24,163
Line 3. q is accepting if and only if h of q is accepting.

30
00:04:24,613 --> 00:04:48,506
The consequence comes by induction on the length of the string w. Line 1 starts the two runs together. Line 2 keeps them together for each letter. So h of the end state at the top is the end state below. Line 3 then gives the same answer. So the two machines have the same language. A homomorphism is a proof that two machines agree.

31
00:04:48,956 --> 00:05:02,631
You can check a homomorphism by hand with a table. Line 2 must hold for every state and every letter. Four states and two letters give eight rows. In each row, compare the two blue columns.

32
00:05:03,081 --> 00:05:17,865
State A. On a, A goes to B, and h of B is B C. Below, h of A is A, and A on a goes to B C. Same. On b, A goes to D, and h of D is D. Below, A on b goes to D. Same.

33
00:05:18,315 --> 00:05:31,307
State B. On a, B goes to D, which maps to D. Below, B C on a goes to D. On b, B goes to C, which maps to B C. Below, B C on b goes to B C.

34
00:05:31,757 --> 00:05:40,823
State C gives the same two rows as B, because C also maps to B C. C on a goes to D, and C on b goes to C.

35
00:05:41,273 --> 00:05:48,484
State D. D goes to D on both letters, and below, D also goes to D. All eight rows agree.

36
00:05:48,934 --> 00:06:02,907
Now lines 1 and 3. h of A is A, the start state. B and C accept and map to B C, which accepts. A and D reject and map to A and D, which reject. So h is a homomorphism.

37
00:06:03,357 --> 00:06:13,021
Here is another D F A for a or a b star. Its states are P, Q, R and S. Q and R accept, and S is the error state.

38
00:06:13,471 --> 00:06:20,746
The shape is different. From R, the letter b goes back to Q. In our first D F A, C looped to itself.

39
00:06:21,196 --> 00:06:29,836
Now take the map P to A, Q and R to B C, and S to D. It maps this machine onto the same minimal machine.

40
00:06:30,286 --> 00:06:40,334
The slides say that every D F A for the language does this. If you minimize two of them, you get the same picture. Only the names of the states are different.

41
00:06:40,784 --> 00:06:46,331
Check the second map with the same table. Eight rows again, one per state and letter.

42
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:58,215
P. On a, P goes to Q, which maps to B C. Below, A on a goes to B C. On b, P goes to S, which maps to D. Below, A on b goes to D.

43
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:09,524
Q. On a, Q goes to S, so D. Below, B C on a goes to D. On b, Q goes to R, which maps to B C. Below, B C loops on b.

44
00:07:09,974 --> 00:07:19,958
R. On a, R goes to S, so D. On b, R goes back to Q, and Q maps to B C. The loop on B C covers this arrow too.

45
00:07:20,408 --> 00:07:24,717
S goes to S on both letters, and below, D goes to D.

46
00:07:25,167 --> 00:07:39,546
P maps to the start state A. Q and R accept, and their image B C accepts. P and S reject, and so do A and D. So this map is also a homomorphism onto the same minimal machine.

47
00:07:39,996 --> 00:07:54,737
The slides add two conditions on the input D F A. Total means that every state has an arrow on every letter, so the error state is drawn. Trim means that every state is reached from the start by some string.

48
00:07:55,187 --> 00:08:05,833
Here the machine has no sink. Q has no arrow on a. So delta of Q and a does not exist, and line 2 has no left side for this state and letter.

49
00:08:06,283 --> 00:08:17,376
Here the machine has a state U that no string reaches. The map gets a state from a string that reaches it. No string reaches U, so h has no rule for U.

50
00:08:17,826 --> 00:08:27,661
So the statement is for trim, total D F As. Each of them maps onto the minimal machine. This is one more reason to draw the error state.

51
00:08:28,111 --> 00:08:36,324
The minimal machine for a or a b star has three states. Could some other D F A for the language get away with two states?

52
00:08:36,774 --> 00:08:48,081
Pick one string that reaches each state of the minimal machine. The empty string, epsilon, reaches A. The string a reaches B C. The string b reaches D.

53
00:08:48,531 --> 00:08:56,829
Now compare them in pairs. Epsilon and a. Add the empty suffix. Then a is in the language, and epsilon is not.

54
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:03,594
Epsilon and b. Add the suffix a. Then the string a is in the language, and b a is not.

55
00:09:04,044 --> 00:09:13,729
a and b. Add the empty suffix. a is in the language, and b is not. So every two of the three strings are told apart by some suffix.

56
00:09:14,179 --> 00:09:20,110
Any D F A for a or a b star reads epsilon, a and b from its start state.

57
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:24,635
Suppose that a D F A for the language has only two states.

58
00:09:25,085 --> 00:09:31,847
Three strings and two states. So two of the strings end in the same state. Say epsilon and a.

59
00:09:32,297 --> 00:09:43,007
After that state, every suffix does the same thing for both strings. The suffix leads to one end state. So the machine accepts both or rejects both.

60
00:09:43,457 --> 00:09:55,958
But the table says that the empty suffix gives a, which is in the language, and epsilon, which is not. That is a contradiction. The same thing happens for each pair of the three strings.

61
00:09:56,408 --> 00:10:13,048
So the three strings end in three different states. Every D F A for the language has at least three states. Ours has exactly three. A python check also tried every two-state D F A over a and b. None of them accepts this language.

62
00:10:13,498 --> 00:10:25,423
The same argument works for any machine that minimization returns. Moore splits two states only when a suffix forces it. So every pair of its states has a separating suffix.

63
00:10:25,873 --> 00:10:44,604
The last slides of the lecture go deeper. They are background for the facts above. Two states p and q are equivalent if no string is accepted from one and rejected from the other. The block of q is the set of states equivalent to q. Here the block of B is B and C.

64
00:10:45,054 --> 00:10:56,019
This relation is a congruence. If p and q are equivalent, then on each letter they go to equivalent states. On a, B and C both go to D.

65
00:10:56,469 --> 00:11:10,293
On b, B goes to C and C goes to C. Both targets are in the block B C. The reason: if a suffix w separated the two targets, then x w would separate p and q.

66
00:11:10,743 --> 00:11:34,935
So we can build the quotient machine. Its states are the blocks. Its start state is the block of the start state. On letter x, the block of q goes to the block of delta of q and x. The choice of q in the block does not matter, because the relation is a congruence. The accepting states are the blocks inside F. This is exactly the minimal machine below.

67
00:11:35,385 --> 00:11:51,321
The theorem. Let M be trim and total. The map that sends each state to its block is a surjective homomorphism onto the quotient. Surjective means that every state of the quotient is hit, because every block is non-empty.

68
00:11:51,771 --> 00:12:10,075
And no D F A for the language has fewer states than the quotient. The algorithm splits only when a suffix forces it, so any D F A for the language must keep those states apart too. The slides name Moore, 1956, and the Myhill Nerode theorem.

69
00:12:10,525 --> 00:12:28,573
The corollary. If two machines have the same language, then their quotients are isomorphic. They are the same machine after a renaming of states. The renaming is unique, because in a trim machine every state is reached by some string, so the map has no choices.

70
00:12:29,023 --> 00:12:42,933
So the minimal D F A is not just a smallest one. It is the smallest one, up to the names of the states. The homomorphisms also tell you which state of one machine does the job of which state of the other.

71
00:12:43,383 --> 00:12:56,929
To summarize. A homomorphism maps the states of one D F A to the states of another. The start goes to the start, each step goes to a step with the same letter, and accepting goes to accepting.

72
00:12:57,379 --> 00:13:05,977
A homomorphism proves that two D F As accept the same strings. You can check one with a table of all states and letters.

73
00:13:06,427 --> 00:13:12,336
Every trim, total D F A for a language maps onto the same minimal D F A.

74
00:13:12,786 --> 00:13:22,151
The minimal D F A is the smallest. Strings that reach different states are told apart by some suffix, so no machine can merge them.

75
00:13:22,601 --> 00:13:33,268
And the minimal D F A is unique up to the names of its states. In part 2 we use this fact to decide if two regexes describe the same language.
